course: medicine
lesson: 67
title: Losing well and winning well
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Straight after a match your body is still full of adrenaline, and anger can fire in a split second while the thinking «brake» part of the brain catches up more slowly, which is why Mason in Texas smashed his racket after losing 6–2 and yelled «Bro, that line judge is sus!» («sus» is young slang for suspicious, dodgy).
explain: Hitting things doesn't let the anger out; studies suggest it tends to keep you angry, while slow breathing out calms the body down, so the trick is to breathe, shake hands, and be cross later in private.
explain: After a loss almost everyone's brain hunts for an excuse that isn't «they were better» (blaming the ref, the shuttle, a teammate); scientists call it a self-serving bias, and noticing it is the first step to learning from the game.
explain: Winning well matters just as much: Ellie's team in Newcastle won 5–0 and she shouted «Yous were rubbish!» at the losers, but Sienna in Melbourne won her race and swam over to the last girl, «Good on ya, ripper effort» («ripper» is Aussie for excellent).
explain: The handshake line is a signal to body and brain that the battle is over: you respect the game and the effort, win or lose, and you celebrate with your own team, never at the other one.
fact: After a match adrenaline stays high and anger can flare before the thinking part of the brain catches up.
fact: Studies suggest that hitting or smashing things tends to keep people angry rather than calm them; slow breathing out helps the body settle.
fact: Blaming the referee, the equipment or a teammate after losing is a common habit called a self-serving bias.
fact: A match is made of many mistakes and good moves by everybody, so one player never loses a game alone.
fact: In a single game luck, bounces and goalkeepers can decide the score, so the scoreboard doesn't always show who played better.
fact: Crying after a big loss is the body letting off pressure, and top players often cry after finals, win or lose.
fact: The handshake line respects the game and the effort; it doesn't mean you are glad you lost.
fact: Walking off without shaking hands feels like relief for seconds, but people remember it for weeks, and many coaches bench players for it.
fact: Rubbing it in after a win upsets the losers, who remember it, and next week you might be the one losing.
fact: When a kids' game is already won by a lot, passing more and trying new positions keeps it fun for both teams; piling on more and more goals makes the losers feel humiliated (small and ashamed), and some may quit.
fact: After losing, it helps to learn one thing from the game and then let it go; replaying a mistake over and over is just worry and can keep you awake.
fact: «Howay» and «yous» are Geordie (Newcastle) for come on and you lot; «lush» is Welsh slang for lovely; «chur» is New Zealand slang for cheers or great.
exercise: Why can anger flare so fast right after losing? | Adrenaline is high and the brain's brake is slower | The body runs out of water during sport | Losing makes your muscles grow bigger | Rackets and balls give off angry chemicals
exercise: Mason smashes his racket «to get the anger out». What do studies suggest? | Hitting things tends to keep you angry | Smashing things is the best way to calm down | Breaking a racket lowers your heart rate | Anger leaves through your hands when you hit
exercise: What calms the body down after a loss? | Slowly breathing out | Shouting at the line judge | Running off the court | Throwing your kit bag
exercise: Ellie shouts «Yous were rubbish!» at the losing team. What's better? | Celebrate with your own team, not at them | Shout it louder so everyone hears | Only shout it if you won by 5 | Write it on the team's group chat so it stays there
exercise: Is shaking hands fake if you lost? | No, it respects the game and the effort | Yes, you should only shake hands if you won the game | Yes, losers should leave at once | No, it means you're glad you lost
exercise: Zara bursts into tears after losing 23–24. Is that OK? | Yes, crying lets off pressure; then shake hands | No, real athletes never cry | Yes, and she can skip the handshake | No, crying makes the score worse
exercise: Harvey blames Josh for the 2–3 loss. What's true? | A match is many players' mistakes and good moves | One player always loses the game alone | Josh lost it because he missed one shot | The best player should take the blame
exercise: Sienna swims over to the girl who came last. What is that? | Winning gracefully | Showing off her medal | Breaking the race rules | Teasing the slowest swimmer
exercise: Noah skates off without the handshake line. What usually happens? | People remember it, and coaches may bench him | The winners feel very sorry for him and let it go | It gets him a fair rematch | Nobody notices it at all
exercise: Aditi loses 15–21 and shouts «you cheated!». What is this called? | A self-serving bias | A fair complaint to the umpire | A sporting rule about line calls | A lucky guess that she was right
exercise: Jack wins 1–0 from an own goal after the others had 12 shots. What's true? | Luck can decide one game, so win humbly | The score always shows which team was the better one | The other team must have cheated | Own goals don't count in the result
exercise: Grace replays her missed penalty all night. What helps? | Learn one thing, then let it go | Replay it a hundred times more | Stay awake until it feels better | Never play a final again
exercise: Leo's under-9s are 12–0 up. What keeps it a game? | Passing more and trying new positions | Shooting from everywhere until they reach 20 | Telling the other team to go home early | Laughing at the other keeper to cheer him up
exercise: Mason lost the set 6–2. How many games behind was he? | 4 | 8 | 2 | 6
exercise: What does «howay» mean in Newcastle? | Come on | Go away | Well done | Hello there
test: «crap ref, we lost 'cause of him, that's it». What's more likely? | The other team simply played better that day | The ref decided the whole game alone, start to finish | Refs always help the home team | Losing is never down to the players
test: Josh's mate: «Harvey's right, Josh missed, it's all on him». The team took 10 shots and scored 2. How many misses? | 8, and only 1 was Josh's | 2, both were Josh's | 10, all Josh's | 12, counting the keeper's saves too
test: What would happen if Noah walked the handshake line even though it stung? | He'd keep the respect of both teams | He'd lose the next match for sure | His coach would bench him for being too soft | The other team would laugh at him
test: In a cartoon the losing hero smashes the trophy and feels better at once. What's real? | Smashing things tends to keep you angry | Smashing things makes you calm at once, like magic | Trophies are made to be smashed | Anger always vanishes after a crash
test: Cian's team mumble «good game, lads» after rugby in Cork. What does the ritual do? | Signals the battle is over, so the body settles | Shows they are glad they lost | Tells the ref to change the score | Starts a second half of the match
test: Zara's coach: «Shame, man» (South African for «oh, poor you») and a hug. Was a cry OK? | Yes, top players cry after finals too | No, crying is only for babies and toddlers | Yes, but only if you won the match in the end | No, crying hurts your lungs while you're puffed
test: Jack: «lush, a win's a win». The other team had 12 shots, his team 2. How many more did they have? | 10 | 14 | 12 | 6
test: Leo: «chur, let's get twenty!» at 12–0. What would happen to the other team if he kept going? | They'd feel humiliated, and some might quit | They'd thank him for the practice | They'd have more fun than ever | Nothing, kids don't mind big losses as long as they play
test: Ellie: «get in, yous were rubbish!» What happens next week if her team loses? | The others will remember and rub it in | Nothing, nobody ever remembers a school match | Her team will win anyway | The losers will invite her to tea
test: Aditi: «that shuttle was out, you cheated!». What's a smarter question to ask herself? | What did she do better, and how can I practise it? | How can I make the next opponent cheat? | Why do all players always cheat me? | Why don't they ban shuttles that go out?
test: Grace can't sleep: «I keep seeing the miss, over and over». Why is she awake? | Stress hormones stay up and the mind keeps rewinding | She ate too much at half-time | Missing a penalty makes legs restless | Her body forgot how to fall asleep forever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which anger may be expressed impulsively immediately following competitive defeat. | Elevated adrenaline precedes the restraint of deliberate thought | Dehydration of the muscles of the arm | An excess of oxygen in the blood | The influence of the opposing team's supporters
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the attribution of a defeat solely to the referee or to equipment? | It frequently reflects a self-serving bias | It is invariably an accurate assessment | It is a requirement of the rules of the game | It is a reliable indicator of cheating
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the outcome of a single match. | Chance may substantially influence the result | The result invariably reflects the superior team | Own goals are excluded from the result | The result is determined before kick-off
exam: Determine the conduct most appropriate for a team whose victory is assured by a considerable margin in a junior fixture. | Distribution of possession and experimentation with positions | Maximisation of the margin of victory | Withdrawal from the field of play | Commentary upon the deficiencies of the opponent
